    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "I3mjbq",
      "x": 2900,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 02 Board",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 400,
      "content": "02 BOARD — every task, under the goal it serves\n\nWHAT IT IS — Left: the goal spine at three levels of abstraction — the standing objective, the goals beneath it, and the breakdown of whichever goal is open. Each level carries its own progress and open-task count, so \"are we getting there?\" is answered at the altitude you ask it. Right: the kanban of live tasks, scoped to the selection in the spine — queued, in progress, waiting on you, done this week. Cards carry the breakdown they belong to, their owner, their id, and what they have cost.\n\nFOLDS IN — the work queue, the old kanban, goals, and the spatial floor view.\n\nDROPPED — the metric tiles (cash, spend, runway), the money card, and the drift banner. Money is a standing setting, so its ceilings live on surface 04; when a ceiling is approached it becomes an inbox item. A board that also reports finance is two surfaces wearing one coat.\n\nNEEDS — the goal tree with progress per node; tasks with status, owner, goal linkage, and accumulated cost; the same waiting-on-you set the inbox draws from.\n\nWRITES — create a task; create a goal; start a queued task; cancel a running one; re-parent a task under a different breakdown.\n\nGUARDRAIL — the waiting-on-you column is a projection of the inbox, not a second queue: it says so on the column, and acting in either place fires the same action. Goals are as deep as three levels; a fourth level means the goal was written as a task."
    },
