    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "x0uO1S",
      "x": 6100,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 04 Authority",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 400,
      "content": "04 AUTHORITY — every standing setting, in one list\n\nWHAT IT IS — One flat, readable list of what the company may and may not do without you: who may act alone, who must ask first, what can never happen, what money may be spent and up to what ceiling, which keys are held by whom and when they expire, and what happens when something goes wrong. Grouped by kind, not by person, because the question is usually \"what needs my word?\" rather than \"what can Sage do?\" — the per-person cut is the panel on surface 01. Each row states its standing, who set it, and when; rows that cannot be changed say so instead of offering a control.\n\nFOLDS IN — per-agent operating policy, capability grants, budget ceilings, credential deposits, escalation rules, and the stop-everything switch.\n\nWHY A LIST — settings hidden behind per-person disclosures cannot be audited by reading. A single list makes the company's whole standing posture legible in one screen, and makes the inbox explicable: every card there points at a row here.\n\nNEEDS — the current grants per subject and capability; ceilings and their period; key deposits with holder, scope and expiry; escalation and pause thresholds; and for each row the identity that set it and when. Rows that are invariant are marked as such by the system, not by convention.\n\nWRITES — change a standing; set or move a ceiling; deposit or revoke a key; adjust an escalation threshold; stop everything. Each write is recorded with who, when, and what it replaced — that record is the drill-down from the row, not a separate surface.\n\nGUARDRAIL — reading this list must be enough to know what the company can do unsupervised. If a permission exists that this list does not show, the list is wrong. Nothing here is presented as the product's pitch; it is what makes the rest safe."
    },
