      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "05 FIRST CONTACT — one question, no forms\n\nWHAT IT IS — The first screen of the product is a person asking you a question. No account wizard, no plan picker, no runtime chooser, no company-details form. Ada introduces herself in one line and asks the only thing she genuinely cannot infer: what are we building. A sentence is a complete answer.\n\nWHY ADA SPEAKS FIRST — The owner should never have to work out what to type. Every screen in this journey opens with Ada already moving: she asks, she drafts, she shows her work. The owner corrects. A blank composer waiting for the user to figure out the next step is the failure mode this replaces.\n\nSKIPPED — Picking a runtime (we connect the best available one and say so only if it fails), naming the company (Ada drafts a name and flags it as a guess), currency (inferred from the answer, changeable in one tap), autonomy tiers, roles, mandates. None of these are questions an owner can answer well before they've seen anything.\n\nNEEDS — one free-text answer; a live executive session; the honest fallback copy for when no runtime can be reached.\n\nGUARDRAIL — No progress bar, no step counter. A counted wizard promises a fixed number of questions; this flow promises the opposite — as few as possible, and Ada decides which are worth asking."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "AZnJc",
      "x": 9300,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 06 The talk",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "06 THE TALK — the budget is made out of a sentence\n\nWHAT IT IS — The conversation on the left, and on the right the things that conversation has created. Every row on the right appeared because of something said on the left. The newest one is marked 'just now' so the owner watches the company assemble itself in real time.\n\nTHE ONE STRUCTURED MOMENT — Money is the single thing collected with a control rather than prose, because a number and a currency should never be parsed out of a sentence. It arrives as a card inside the conversation, pre-filled with Ada's read of what the owner said, with the amount already typed in. Confirming is one tap; correcting is one tap.\n\nADA LEADS — There is no user turn between Ada recording the mission and Ada asking about money. She acknowledges, records, and moves on by herself. The owner never has to say 'what's next'.\n\nNEEDS — the running draft (purpose, first goal, money); which item changed most recently; a per-row change affordance that reopens the conversation rather than a form.\n\nGUARDRAIL — The right panel is a preview, not a record: 'Nothing is live yet' stays on it until the signature. Every row is reversible by asking, and says so."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "sZXJ7",
      "x": 10900,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 07 The team",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "07 THE TEAM — people made out of a sentence\n\nWHAT IT IS — The same shell, one step later. Ada proposes a team unprompted, as a card of four people with plain-language lanes — 'Finding cafés', 'Orders & deliveries' — not roles, mandates, runtimes or permissions. The right panel gains the team as a row with four faces and 'waiting on you'.\n\nADA LEADS — The owner did not ask 'who do I need?'. Ada finishes the money question and moves to staffing on her own, and says why these four for this business. An owner who has never run a company cannot be expected to know what to ask for; that is the whole point of having an executive.\n\nEACH PERSON IS DROPPABLE — One 'drop' per row, and 'Change it' for the shape of the whole team. Editing a person's brief happens by talking, not by opening a form.\n\nNEEDS — a proposed team with name, lane and a one-line brief per member; the ability to remove one; the same 'nothing is hired yet' state as everything else on the panel.\n\nGUARDRAIL — No runtime names, no model names, no skill lists, no permission matrices anywhere on this screen. Those exist, and they live on surface 04 after the company is running."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "CN9Dx",
      "x": 12500,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 08 One last look",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "08 ONE LAST LOOK — the only signature in the whole journey\n\nWHAT IT IS — Everything Ada drafted, on one page, every field editable in place. What is on this page is exactly what gets created — the owner corrects here rather than talking in circles. One press-and-hold starts the company and everyone in it.\n\nAUTHORITY IN PLAIN WORDS — The operating boundary is shown as two columns an owner can actually read: 'On her own' and 'Asks you first', with real examples and real amounts. No tiers, no scopes, no grants. The underlying setting is the same one surface 04 lists; only the language differs.\n\nWHY HOLD, NOT CLICK — The hold is the product's one gesture of consent, used here first so it is familiar everywhere after. It is deliberate, it cannot be fired by a stray click, and it is the same gesture that later approves an email or a payment.\n\nNEEDS — the full draft; inline edit for every field; the two authority columns derived from the same setting the company will run under; a clear 'nothing has been created yet' state.\n\nGUARDRAIL — This is the only signature in onboarding. If a second confirmation appears after this one, the flow is wrong: the owner was told this page was the decision."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "MadaS",
      "x": 14100,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 09 Day one",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "09 DAY ONE — the agent you met is the agent you keep\n\nWHAT IT IS — The real app, opened on surface 01, with the onboarding conversation still in it. Above the divider is the last thing Ada said while setting up; below it the company is live and she keeps talking. The thread is not archived, replayed or summarised — it is the same thread, and Ada is now the first row of your chat list.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS — The onboarding agent becoming a permanent colleague is what makes setup feel like hiring rather than configuring. There is no hand-off to a different assistant, no 'setup complete' dead end, and no second inbox: the four people Ada proposed are now in the directory beneath her, each with a status line saying what they started doing.\n\nADA STILL LEADS — Her first message after launch is work, not a welcome: she has found 34 cafés and Pip has drafted the first five approaches. The owner's first action in the product is a judgement call, which is the job they actually signed up for.\n\nNEEDS — the graduated session rendered as thread history; the created staff with live status; Ada's authority summary in the same plain words used at signing; spend against the ceiling from minute one.\n\nGUARDRAIL — The divider must be a real event with a time, not decoration. If the conversation cannot be carried into the company, the promise of the whole journey breaks."
    },
