    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "DkThI",
      "x": 15700,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 10 The hand-off",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "10 THE HAND-OFF — who asked whom, inside the company\n\nWHAT IT IS — A detail view of one task, reached by opening a card on the board. It answers the question an owner actually has about a team they cannot see: who is doing this, and who decided that. Ada took the goal, split it four ways, and stays answerable for all of it. Each hand-off is one row: the person, the ask in one plain line, its state, what it has cost, and a way into their page.\n\nDEPTH IS REAL — Pip asked Sal a question mid-task, and that sub-ask is indented under Pip with its own spine. An agent asking a colleague is ordinary work, not an exception, so it is rendered as ordinary structure rather than an event in a log. Sal's answer is shown inline, because the answer is the point.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS — Delegation is where an autonomous company either earns or loses trust. If the owner cannot see that Pip checked capacity with Sal before pitching fourteen more accounts, the company looks like a black box that got lucky.\n\nNEEDS — the delegation graph for a task: parent and child work, who asked, when, current state, accumulated cost per branch, and which branch is blocked on which. The 'waiting on you' item is the same one the inbox holds.\n\nGUARDRAIL — This is a view of one task's tree, not a global org chart or a fleet console. Every row's cost is real recorded spend, and the panel states plainly when nothing has left the company yet."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "AxS3q",
      "x": 17300,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 11 Their page · work",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "11 THEIR PAGE · WORK — one agent, everything about them\n\nWHAT IT IS — The profile of a single employee, opened from a row in People or from any hand-off row. Same shape whoever you open: header, three tabs, and a panel of money and rules that never changes as you move between tabs. This is the 'work' tab: what they are doing this second, their own board, and the hand-offs in and out.\n\nTHEIR OWN BOARD — Every employee has their own queue, not just a slice of a company board. Columns match surface 02 so nothing has to be relearned, and 'waiting on you' is the same item the inbox holds, in the place the work was done.\n\nMONEY IS ALWAYS VISIBLE — The panel shows what this one person has spent this month against the slice their manager gave them, broken into thinking, looking things up, and anything sent outside. An agent with a ceiling it cannot cross is the difference between staff and a runaway process, so the ceiling is stated on their own page, not buried in settings.\n\nNEEDS — current task with elapsed time and cost; the employee's own task list by state; incoming and outgoing delegations; per-employee spend against their allocation with a category split; the per-person authority summary; who they answer to.\n\nGUARDRAIL — Plain words only: 'Finding cafés', not a role string; 'thinking', not token cost; no model names, no prompts, no skill lists. The full permission list is one link away on surface 04, where it belongs."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "bsZml",
      "x": 18900,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 12 Their page · chats",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "12 THEIR PAGE · CHATS — the conversations you were never in\n\nWHAT IT IS — Every thread this employee is part of, split into the one with you and the ones with colleagues. Opening one shows a normal group conversation between two of your staff — both sides left-aligned with their own avatar, because neither of them is you.\n\nWHY SHOW IT AT ALL — The company keeps working while the owner is away, and most of that work is colleagues talking. Pip checking capacity with Sal before pitching is exactly the judgement an owner would want to have happened. Hiding it would make the company feel like it guessed; showing it makes delegation legible without requiring supervision.\n\nYOU CAN JOIN, YOU DON'T HAVE TO — There is a composer, and the line under it says the truth: they carry on without you, this is here so you can look. Unread counts exist on colleague threads so the owner can find what changed, not so they owe anyone a reply.\n\nNEEDS — per-employee thread list with unread state; agent-to-agent messages with author, time, and the task they belong to; the outcome markers where a conversation created work ('added T-21 on Sal's board') or escalated ('raised the oven question with Ada').\n\nGUARDRAIL — These are conversations, not logs. If a thread reads like machine output rather than two colleagues talking, the underlying interaction is wrong and the UI should not paper over it."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "MKba5",
      "x": 20500,
      "y": 1560,
      "name": "DOC 13 Their page · workspace",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 470,
      "content": "13 THEIR PAGE · WORKSPACE — where the work actually is\n\nWHAT IT IS — The employee's own copy of the work: the files they have made, the one currently in front of you, and what they changed today. A draft is shown as the thing it is — an email a café would read — not as a diff, an artifact id, or a version record.\n\nTHE LAST STEP BEFORE THE OUTSIDE WORLD — The open draft carries the same amber 'waiting on you' state used everywhere else, and the same press-and-hold gesture used at founding. The note under the button says exactly what the hold does: this is the only step between the draft and a real café. Approval sends; nothing else does.\n\nCHANGED TODAY — Each line says what changed in the owner's language ('rewrote the opening after Sal's capacity note'), which ties the file back to the conversation on the chats tab and the hand-off on surface 10. The three views are the same story told at three depths.\n\nNEEDS — the employee's workspace tree; file contents rendered as their real form; per-file authorship and update time; the pending review state and its approval action; a change list with plain-language summaries.\n\nGUARDRAIL — Never present a workspace as a code editor or a file manager. The owner is reviewing work, not browsing a disk; if a file cannot be shown as the thing it will become, it should not be the thing on screen."
    },
